The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
if (of_phy_is_fixed_link(pdev->dev.of_node)) {
    ret = of_phy_register_fixed_link(pdev->dev.of_node);
    if (ret < 0) {
        dev_err(&pdev->dev, "bad fixed-link spec\n");
        goto err_free_bus;
    }
}
```
Reason:
The code checks if the device tree node specifies a fixed-link PHY and registers it. However, if an error occurs later in the probe process (e.g., during MII bus registration), the fixed-link PHY is not deregistered, leading to a memory leak since the allocated resources for the fixed-link PHY are not released.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
if (of_phy_is_fixed_link(pdev->dev.of_node))
    of_phy_deregister_fixed_link(pdev->dev.of_node);
```
Reason:
These statements are added to ensure that if the probe process fails after registering the fixed-link PHY, it will be properly deregistered, thus preventing memory leaks by releasing the resources allocated for the fixed-link PHY. This is also done in the `nb8800_remove` function to ensure cleanup when the driver is removed.

